## sn5.9
sn5.9:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn5.9:1.2 | S: Then the nun Selā robed up in the morning … | T: Then, in the morning, the bhikkhuni Sela dressed …
sn5.9:1.3 | S: and sat at the root of a tree for the day’s meditation. | T: she sat down at the foot of a tree for the day’s abiding.
sn5.9:1.4 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, wanting to make the nun Selā feel fear, terror, and goosebumps … | T: Then Mara the Evil One, desiring to arouse fear, trepidation, and terror in the bhikkhuni Sela, desiring to make her fall away from concentration,
sn5.9:1.5 | S: addressed her in verse: | T: approached her and addressed her in verse:
sn5.9:2.1 | S: “Who created this puppet? | T: By whom has this puppet been created?
sn5.9:2.2 | S: Where is its maker? | T: Where is the maker of the puppet?
sn5.9:2.3 | S: Where has the puppet arisen? | T: Where has the puppet arisen?
sn5.9:2.4 | S: And where does it cease?” | T: Where does the puppet cease?”
sn5.9:3.1 | S: Then the nun Selā thought, | T: Then it occurred to the bhikkhuni Sela:
sn5.9:3.2 | S: “Who’s speaking this verse, a human or a non-human?” | T: “Now who is this…?
sn5.9:3.3 | S: Then she thought, | T: (none)
sn5.9:3.4 | S: “This is Māra the Wicked, wanting to make me feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, wanting to make me fall away from immersion!” | T: This is Mara the Evil One … desiring to make me fall away from concentration.”
sn5.9:3.5 | S: Then Selā, knowing that this was Māra the Wicked, replied to him in verse: | T: Then the bhikkhuni Sela, having understood, “This is Mara the Evil One,” replied to him in verses:
sn5.9:4.1 | S: “This puppet isn’t self-made, | T: “This puppet is not made by itself,
sn5.9:4.2 | S: nor is this gloom made by another. | T: Nor is this misery made by another.
sn5.9:4.3 | S: It comes to be because of a cause, | T: It has come to be dependent on a cause;
sn5.9:4.4 | S: and ceases when the cause breaks up. | T: With the cause’s breakup it will cease.
sn5.9:5.1 | S: It’s like a seed that’s sown | T: “As when a seed is sown in a field
sn5.9:5.2 | S: in a field; it grows | T: It grows depending on a pair of factors:
sn5.9:5.3 | S: relying on both the soil’s nutrients | T: It requires both the soil’s nutrients
sn5.9:5.4 | S: as well as moisture. | T: And a steady supply of moisture:
sn5.9:6.1 | S: In the same way the aggregates and elements | T: “Just so the aggregates and elements,
sn5.9:6.2 | S: and these six sense fields | T: And these six bases of sensory contact,
sn5.9:6.3 | S: come to be because of a cause, | T: Have come to be dependent on a cause;
sn5.9:6.4 | S: and cease when the cause breaks up.” | T: With the cause’s breakup they will cease.”
sn5.9:7.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The nun Selā knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Mara the Evil One, realizing, “The bhikkhuni Sela knows me,” sad and disappointed, disappeared right there.

## an3.61
an3.61:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these three sectarian tenets—as pursued, pressed, and grilled by the astute—when taken to their conclusion, end with inaction. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these three sectarian tenets which, when questioned, interrogated, and cross-examined by the wise, and taken to their conclusion, will eventuate in non-doing.
an3.61:1.2 | S: What three? | T: What are the three?
an3.61:1.3 | S: There are some ascetics and brahmins who have this doctrine and view: | T: (1) “There are, bhikkhus, some ascetics and brahmins who hold such a doctrine and view as this:
an3.61:1.4 | S: ‘Everything this individual person experiences—pleasurable, painful, or neutral—is because of past deeds.’ | T: ‘Whatever this person experiences—whether pleasure, pain, or neither-pain-nor-pleasure—all that is caused by what was done in the past.’
an3.61:1.5 | S: There are some ascetics and brahmins who have this doctrine and view: | T: (2) There are other ascetics and brahmins who hold such a doctrine and view as this:
an3.61:1.6 | S: ‘Everything this individual person experiences—pleasurable, painful, or neutral—is because of God Almighty’s creation.’ | T: ‘Whatever this person experiences—whether pleasure, pain, or neither-pain-nor-pleasure—all that is caused by God’s creative activity.’
an3.61:1.7 | S: There are some ascetics and brahmins who have this doctrine and view: | T: (3) And there are still other ascetics and brahmins who hold such a doctrine and view as this:
an3.61:1.8 | S: ‘Everything this individual person experiences—pleasurable, painful, or neutral—has no cause or reason.’ | T: ‘Whatever this person experiences—whether pleasure, pain, or neither-pain-nor-pleasure—all that occurs without a cause or condition.’
an3.61:2.1 | S: Regarding this, I went up to the ascetics and brahmins whose view is that | T: (1) “Bhikkhus, I approached those ascetics and brahmins who hold such a doctrine and view as this:
an3.61:2.2 | S: everything an individual person experiences is because of past deeds, and I said to them: | T: ‘Whatever this person experiences—whether pleasure, pain, or neither-pain-nor-pleasure—all that is caused by past deeds,’ and I said to them:
an3.61:2.3 | S: ‘Is it really true that this is the venerables’ view?’ | T: ‘Is it true that you venerable ones hold such a doctrine and view?’
an3.61:2.4 | P: “yaṁ kiñcāyaṁ purisapuggalo paṭisaṁvedeti sukhaṁ vā dukkhaṁ vā adukkhamasukhaṁ vā sabbaṁ taṁ pubbekatahetū”’ti? | T: (none)
an3.61:2.5 | S: And they answered, ‘Yes’. | T: When I ask them this, they affirm it.
an3.61:2.6 | S: I said to them: | T: Then I say to them:
an3.61:2.7 | S: ‘In that case, you might kill living creatures, steal, be unchaste; use speech that’s false, backbiting, harsh, or nonsensical; be covetous, malicious, or have wrong view, all because of past deeds.’ | T: ‘In such a case, it is due to past deeds that you might destroy life, take what is not given, indulge in sexual activity, speak falsehood, utter divisive speech, speak harshly, indulge in idle chatter; that you might be full of longing, have a mind of ill will, and hold wrong view.’
an3.61:3.1 | S: Those who believe that past deeds are the most important thing have no enthusiasm or effort, no idea that there are things that should and should not be done. | T: “Those who fall back on past deeds as the essential truth have no desire to do what should be done and to avoid doing what should not be done, nor do they make an effort in this respect.
an3.61:3.2 | S: Not finding as a genuine fact that there are things that should and should not be done, they’re unmindful and careless, and can’t rightly be called ascetics. | T: Since they do not apprehend as true and valid anything that should be done or should not be done, they are muddle-minded, they do not guard themselves, and even the personal designation ‘ascetic’ could not be legitimately applied to them.
an3.61:3.3 | S: This is my first legitimate refutation of the ascetics and brahmins who have this doctrine and view. | T: This was my first legitimate refutation of those ascetics and brahmins who hold such a doctrine and view.
an3.61:4.1 | S: Regarding this, I went up to the ascetics and brahmins whose view is that | T: (2) “Then, bhikkhus, I approached those ascetics and brahmins who hold such a doctrine and view as this:
an3.61:4.2 | S: everything that an individual person experiences is because of God Almighty’s creation, and I said to them: | T: ‘Whatever this person experiences—whether pleasure, pain, or neither-pain-nor-pleasure—all that is caused by God’s creative activity,’ and I said to them:
an3.61:4.3 | S: ‘Is it really true that this is the venerables’ view?’ | T: ‘Is it true that you venerable ones hold such a doctrine and view?’
an3.61:4.4 | P: yaṁ kiñcāyaṁ purisapuggalo paṭisaṁvedeti sukhaṁ vā dukkhaṁ vā adukkhamasukhaṁ vā sabbaṁ taṁ issaranimmānahetū’ti? | T: (none)
an3.61:4.5 | S: And they answered, ‘Yes’. | T: When I ask them this, they affirm it.
an3.61:4.6 | S: I said to them: | T: Then I say to them:
an3.61:4.7 | S: ‘In that case, you might kill living creatures, steal, be unchaste; use speech that’s false, backbiting, harsh, or nonsensical; be covetous, malicious, or have wrong view, all because of God Almighty’s creation.’ | T: ‘In such a case, it is due to God’s creative activity that you might destroy life … and hold wrong view.’
an3.61:5.1 | S: Those who believe that God Almighty’s creative power is the most important thing have no enthusiasm, no effort, no idea that there are things that should and should not be done. | T: “Those who fall back on God’s creative activity as the essential truth have no desire to do what should be done and to avoid doing what should not be done, nor do they make an effort in this respect.
an3.61:5.2 | S: Not finding as a genuine fact that there are things that should and should not be done, they’re unmindful and careless, and can’t rightly be called ascetics. | T: Since they do not apprehend as true and valid anything that should be done or should not be done, they are muddle-minded, they do not guard themselves, and even the personal designation ‘ascetic’ could not be legitimately applied to them.
an3.61:5.3 | S: This is my second legitimate refutation of the ascetics and brahmins who have this doctrine and view. | T: This was my second legitimate refutation of those ascetics and brahmins who hold such a doctrine and view.
an3.61:6.1 | S: Regarding this, I went up to the ascetics and brahmins whose view is that | T: (3) “Then, bhikkhus, I approached those ascetics and brahmins who hold such a doctrine and view as this:
